          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "It arrives as other people’s opinions about you",
            "body": "<p>You do not notice this happening to your face. You notice it happening in other people’s mouths. “Late night?” — from a colleague at 9am, after eight hours of sleep. “Everything alright at home?” — from a brother-in-law, genuinely concerned.</p><p>At work, people conclude things about your energy before you open your mouth. On video calls the overhead lighting makes it dramatically worse. At home you get read as checked-out or annoyed when you are neither — and underneath it there is a thing you cannot say out loud: I looked in the mirror and did not recognise the person looking back. He looked worn down. I was not.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "What I tried, in order, and what it cost",
            "body": "<p><strong>Sleep.</strong> Eight hours a night for two months. It helped the morning puffiness slightly and did nothing about the shadow or the texture. <strong>Water and cutting back on drinking.</strong> Good for other reasons; did not touch this. <strong>An eye cream marketed at men</strong> — <strong>$45</strong>, black bottle, exactly the same amount of nothing. <strong>A metal roller</strong> — <strong>$22</strong>, forgotten in a drawer within a fortnight.</p><p>Then I looked at what the actual fix costs. Lower eyelid surgery: <strong>$3,000 to $6,000</strong>, general anaesthetic, and a week to ten days you cannot go to work looking like that. So I did what men do, which is nothing, for another eighteen months — because there was no in-between option.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "The answer, from a dermatologist’s assistant, in about ninety seconds",
            "body": "<p>The outer layer of your skin — the stratum corneum — is a barrier, and keeping things out is its entire job. So when you rub a cream on, most of the active never gets through. Under the eye it is worse, because that skin is the thinnest on the body, so eye products get formulated weak on purpose.</p><p>Weak formula. Strong barrier. Thinnest skin on the body. “You didn’t buy the wrong product,” she said. “Almost nothing in any of them ever arrived.” The problem was never what was in the bottle. It was that it never got in.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r4": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "I looked it up myself that night",
            "body": "<p>Because I was not going to take a corridor conversation as fact. There is published research on each part of this: that the skin barrier blocks most topically applied actives [CITATION 1 — barrier function]; that lower-eyelid skin is the thinnest on the body [CITATION 2 — skin thickness]; and — the load-bearing one — that dissolving microneedles deliver substantially more active into skin than the same active applied on top [CITATION 3 — microneedle vs topical].</p><p>Also that the microneedles fully dissolve with an established tolerability profile [CITATION 4], and that visible change takes roughly six to eight weeks because of skin turnover [CITATION 5]. Not new science — which was the part I found reassuring.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r5": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "What it is, and what it is not",
            "body": "<p>When she said “microneedle” I pictured the spiked roller that draws blood. That is not this. A dissolving microneedle patch contains no metal — the cones on the underside are made from the actives themselves, freeze-dried and compressed into solid points shorter than a grain of salt. They do not reach nerve endings, they do not draw blood, and over a few hours they dissolve completely on the far side of the barrier that stopped every bottle you ever bought.</p><p>Inside the cones: hyaluronic acid (the immediate effect), niacinamide (tone and barrier over weeks), peptides and collagen (firmness, the slow work), vitamin C (dullness) and ceramides (rebuild the barrier that thins with age). A fresh patch is dry to the touch, because the patch is the dose — not a serum on an adhesive.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r6": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "Eight weeks, written down as it happened",
            "body": "<p><strong>Night 1:</strong> a slight prickle for a minute, then nothing; the morning looked flatter and I assumed I was seeing what I wanted to. <strong>Week 1:</strong> real but inconsistent. <strong>Week 3:</strong> the puffiness cleared earlier in the morning than it used to. <strong>Week 5:</strong> a colleague asked if I had been on holiday. <strong>Week 7:</strong> I had stopped angling my laptop up before calls.</p><p>Precision matters: I do not have the eyes I had at thirty-five, and I still have some morning fullness that is structural. What changed is the reading. Nobody has asked me if I was up late in four months. The face stopped filing reports I did not authorise.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r7": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "What I wanted to know before I ordered",
            "body": "<p><strong>Is anyone going to be able to tell?</strong> No — there is no colour in it and nothing sits on your face during the day. <strong>Is this going to turn into a routine?</strong> Two nights a week, on before bed, off in the morning; nothing to layer. <strong>Does it hurt?</strong> A prickle for the first minute, then you stop noticing.</p><p><strong>My bags are genetic.</strong> Then read this carefully: inherited fat pads only surgery shifts. What responds is fluid, thinning skin, lost firmness and texture. Most men are a mix. <strong>Why has no doctor mentioned this?</strong> Appointments are short and triaged toward medical problems — it is a fifteen-minute conversation, not a conspiracy.</p>"
          }
        },
